\description{
Compute localized orthogonal PLQR moments
\deqn{
  \mathbb{E}\!\left[
    \left(\tau - \mathbf{1}\{Y \le D^\top \theta + g_0(U)\}\right)
    \left(D - m_D(U)\right)
    \;\middle|\; X \approx x_0
  \right]
}
at a supplied coefficient value \code{theta_eval}, using DNN rank weights at one
or multiple target points. When positive threshold density values are
supplied through \code{density} or \code{density_col}, the same fit reports the
direct PLQR coefficient sandwich used for standard errors and confidence
intervals. No local root solving is performed: \code{theta} in the result is the
supplied evaluation value (\code{meta$theta_source = "supplied"}).
}
\details{
The documented sampling framework uses independent observations; cluster
and survey-design inference are not supplied. Observations with exactly
equal computed distance to a target share their block's average rank weight,
even when their covariates differ. Groups are target-specific and use exact
represented equality, without a tolerance. Ordering uses distance then input
row index; outcomes and nuisances do not define groups. Every jackknife
deletion averages the surviving blocks with the sample-size-n-1 rule.
A warning is emitted once per public call with distance ties: the accompanying
theory assumes a continuous distance distribution and does not establish
inference under persistent ties. Earlier retained fit schemas require refitting.

Two interfaces are supported.
\enumerate{
\item Data-frame interface (recommended): pass \code{data} with \code{x_cols}, \code{D_cols},
\code{g_col}, \code{mD_cols}, and \code{y_col}.
\item Matrix interface: pass \code{X}, \code{D}, \code{g}, \code{mD}, and \code{Y}.
}

With \code{data}, omit \code{X}, \code{D}, \code{g}, \code{mD}, and \code{Y}; these direct inputs are
reserved for the matrix interface. Column selectors require \code{data}.
A direct \code{density} vector is supported with either interface.

Supply the shape component \code{g}, residualized-regressor means \code{mD} and,
for coefficient uncertainty, positive threshold densities. These can use
adjustment variables beyond the reporting covariates. The threshold is
\verb{D' theta_eval + g}; \code{g} alone is not a conditional quantile.

Coefficient uncertainty requires \code{normalize = TRUE} and a density-backed
Jacobian. \code{theta_meat} chooses analytic score covariance or the full
jackknife covariance of the localized moment. \code{moment_se} alone is not a
coefficient standard error. For statistical background and conditions,
see the \href{https://jakobjuergens.com/research/#orthogonal-inference-conditional-z}{paper}.

A Jacobian, its inverse, or the assembled sandwich covariance outside the
numeric range leaves coefficient uncertainty unavailable with rescaling
guidance. Supplied coefficients and evaluated moments remain available.
Finite singular Jacobians also leave coefficient uncertainty unavailable.

As in \code{\link[=weights_dnn]{weights_dnn()}}, exact DNN localization uses the full rank permutation
at each target rather than an \code{s}-nearest-neighbor truncation. The \code{"fast"}
jackknife option reuses the full-sample rank matrix and updates ranks under
deletion. For exact DNN fits, the unaveraged rank-table support edge is rank
\code{n - s + k}, not rank \code{s}.

Averaging can extend nonzero observation weights through the end of a
distance block that straddles the nominal rank-table edge. Effective sample
sizes and weight diagnostics use the resulting observation weights.

\code{tuning_mode = "default"} evaluates only the default kernel order and
preserves the ordinary scalar-fit return shape. \code{tuning_mode = "grid"}
evaluates the feasible candidates in \code{tuning}, or the paper default tuning
grid when \code{tuning} is omitted, and attaches a tidy \code{tuning_grid} payload.
The payload stores one long-form row per candidate, target, and PLQR moment
component; the public \code{moment} and \code{moment_se} matrix fields remain anchored
to the default candidate. Candidate diagnostics include \code{ess}, \code{max_weight},
\code{top_n}, \code{top5_weight_share}, and \code{degenerate}; \code{top_n} is fixed at 5 and
is independent of the estimator's \code{k}. Direct PLQR sandwich inference from
\code{density} or \code{density_col} currently uses \code{tuning_mode = "default"} only.
}
\section{Lifecycle}{

\strong{Experimental.} The PLQR API and inference interfaces may change.
}

